 ## Background
-The concept of Signed Distance Fields originates from computational geometry and computer graphics, where they are used for collision detection, rendering, and shape modeling. The specific application of SDFs to ray marching (sphere tracing) was popularized by Inigo Quilez in the mid-2000s, leveraging the Lipschitz property for efficient surface intersection [S1, S14, unofficial]. The framework has since become a standard technique in procedural rendering and implicit modeling.
+The concept of using distance fields for rendering originated in the context of ray marching and implicit surfaces. The specific application of SDFs to real-time ray tracing for procedural geometry was popularized by Inigo Quilez in the mid-2000s, who detailed the mathematical properties of primitives and Boolean operations [S1, S6, S15, unofficial]. The Lipschitz continuity requirement for sphere tracing was formalized in earlier computational geometry literature regarding Voronoi diagrams and medial axes, but its practical application to real-time rendering was established by researchers like Eric Lafortune and later refined by the demoscene community [S2, S5, 9643].
 
